    "slug": "write-a-vendor-follow-up-cadence-for-a-philippines-based-operations-team",
    "title": "Write a vendor follow-up cadence for a Philippines-based operations team",
    "excerpt": "A vendor follow-up cadence with named states, message steps, one owner per thread, and an escalation rule that does not depend on memory.",
    "minutes": 6,
    "publicationDate": "2026-11-07",
    "publishedAt": "2026-11-07",
    "image": "/daily07/outsourcedlabor/write-a-vendor-follow-up-cadence-for-a-philippines-based-operations-team.png",
    "body": [
      "## Start from the vendor's response pattern",
      "A follow-up cadence should match how the vendor actually answers, not how the contract says it should. Pull the last twenty threads for one vendor and record when each reply arrived. Note the channel, the person who replied, and the reason for any silence. The pattern gives you a realistic first interval instead of an optimistic guess.",
      "Group the threads by request type because a quote, a delivery date, and a missing invoice do not follow the same rhythm. A vendor may answer delivery questions in a day and finance questions in a week. Use the slower pattern for the escalation trigger. A cadence built on the fastest request type will escalate everything else too early.",
      "## Define the follow-up states",
      "Give every thread a state that tells the specialist what to do next. Useful states include awaiting first reply, awaiting document, promised date, escalated, and closed. Avoid states that describe feelings, such as waiting too long, because they cannot be measured. A small set of states is easier to apply than a long list of labels.",
      "Write the allowed move from each state and the date it should happen. From awaiting first reply, the next move might be a second message after two business days. From promised date, the next move is a check on the morning the date arrives. The state and the move together replace a specialist's memory.",
      "## Write the message ladder",
      "Write three short messages for each request type: a first follow-up, a second follow-up, and an escalation notice. Keep the tone factual and include the original request, the date, and the specific item still missing. Do not repeat the whole history in every message. The vendor should be able to act from the latest note alone.",
      "Make the escalation message name the consequence without inventing a threat. It can say that the order will be held, the payment will wait, or the account owner will be copied. Use a consistent subject line so the thread stays together. A clear ladder reduces the urge to send an angry message that restarts the conversation.",
      "## Assign one owner per vendor thread",
      "One vendor thread should have one owner at a time. The owner may change at a shift handoff, but the log should show the change and the next action. Two specialists following the same vendor creates duplicate messages and conflicting promises. The vendor learns that nobody is really in charge.",
      "Put the owner's name and the next follow-up date beside the thread. During handoff, the outgoing owner updates the state rather than writing a paragraph of context. The incoming owner should be able to act without reading the entire history. Ownership is a field, not a feeling of responsibility. A named owner also makes the handoff visible when the regular contact is on leave.",
      "## Record promises in one shared log",
      "Every vendor promise should land in the same log, even when it arrives by phone. Record the date promised, the item, the person who promised it, and the source message. A promise that lives only in a chat thread disappears at the next shift change. The log is the team's memory for what the vendor owes.",
      "Review the log at the start of each shift and mark the items due today. Do not let the log become a second inbox with no owner. Each line needs a state and a next action. A short log that is checked twice a day is more useful than a complete archive nobody opens.",
      "## Escalate when the cadence stalls",
      "Set an escalation trigger that does not depend on frustration. Examples include two missed promised dates, no reply after the second follow-up, or a missing document that blocks a customer promise. The trigger should name the state and the number of attempts. When it fires, the specialist moves the thread to the account owner with the log attached.",
      "The account owner needs a decision question, not the whole thread. State what is blocked, what the vendor promised, and what the team needs by when. Give the owner two options if possible. An escalation that asks for a decision gets a faster answer than one that asks for sympathy.",
      "## Review the cadence monthly",
      "Review the cadence monthly by vendor and request type. Count the threads that closed on the first promise, the threads that needed a second follow-up, and the threads that escalated. Look for a request type that always stalls. A pattern is easier to fix than a single difficult thread. Bring one stalled thread as an example so the discussion stays concrete.",
      "Ask the vendor's account contact for one process change rather than a general improvement. It might be a shared portal, a named backup, or a required reference number. Record the change and the date it takes effect. Review the same counts next month to see whether the change helped. If the contact cannot name a change, ask for a named backup instead.",
      "## Retire vendors that never respond",
      "Some vendors will not improve, and the cadence should make that visible. If a vendor misses every trigger for two review cycles, prepare a documented case for the business owner. Include the dates, the blocked work, and the replacement cost. Do not keep a vendor in the cadence just because switching feels inconvenient.",
      "A retirement decision belongs to the business owner, not the specialist. The specialist's job is to keep the evidence clean and the work moving. If the owner decides to stay, record the accepted risk and the new expectation. A cadence that never reaches a decision becomes unpaid administrative work. The specialist should keep serving the account while the owner considers the case."
    ]
