    "slug": "set-up-a-daily-queue-reconciliation-for-a-delegated-inbox",
    "title": "Set up a daily queue reconciliation for a delegated inbox",
    "excerpt": "A short daily reconciliation that matches inbox items to source records, catches duplicates, and hands off unresolved work with a reason.",
    "minutes": 6,
    "publicationDate": "2026-11-07",
    "publishedAt": "2026-11-07",
    "image": "/daily07/outsourcedlabor/set-up-a-daily-queue-reconciliation-for-a-delegated-inbox.png",
    "body": [
      "## Reconcile the queue, not just the inbox",
      "An inbox can look clean while the queue is wrong. Messages may be archived without a matching record, or a record may exist with no owner. Reconciliation compares the two views and explains every difference. The goal is not a zero inbox. The goal is a queue where every open item has a record, an owner, and a next action.",
      "Run the check against the system that holds the work, not against a folder name. If orders live in a commerce tool and messages live in a shared inbox, compare by order identifier. When the two systems cannot be matched, the item belongs in an exception list. A reconciliation without a source record is only a count.",
      "## Pick a fixed reconciliation window",
      "Choose a window that matches the work, such as the previous business day or the last twenty-four hours. Keep the window the same every day so the numbers can be compared. A rolling window that changes with the shift makes a spike hard to interpret. Write the window at the top of the reconciliation sheet.",
      "Run the check at a consistent time and name the person responsible. The check should not wait for the busiest person to be free. If the queue runs across time zones, state which time zone defines the day. A daily ritual with a fixed boundary is easier to hand off than an informal sweep.",
      "## Match each item to its source record",
      "For every open item, record the source system, the record identifier, and the owner. If the item has no record, create one before doing the work. If the record has no item, ask whether the customer was already helped outside the inbox. Matching is the step that turns a pile of messages into a managed queue.",
      "Use a short list of match outcomes: matched, missing record, missing message, duplicate, and out of scope. Do not write a paragraph for each item. The outcome tells the next person what to do. A queue with clear outcomes is easier to audit than one with long free-text notes. Add one example outcome to the guide so a new person can copy the format.",
      "## Record the four queue states",
      "Give every open item one of four states: new, in progress, waiting, or blocked. New means no owner has started. In progress means work is active. Waiting means the team needs something from the customer or vendor. Blocked means the item cannot move without a decision. These states show where work actually sits.",
      "Do not use waiting as a hiding place for work nobody wants to do. A waiting item needs a follow-up date and a named owner. A blocked item needs a decision question and an escalation path. Review the counts by state each day and ask why any state grew. A stable queue has movement between states, not just a smaller total.",
      "## Handle duplicates before they multiply",
      "Duplicates are expensive because two people can promise different things to the same customer. When the reconciliation finds a duplicate, choose one record as the source of truth and link the other to it. Tell the customer once, not twice. Close the duplicate with a note that points to the surviving record.",
      "Look for duplicates by customer, order, and subject rather than by exact message text. A customer may write twice with different words. A vendor may forward the same thread with a new subject line. The reconciliation should catch these patterns before the team answers both. Check the customer's open records before sending a second reply on the same subject.",
      "## Hand off unresolved items with a reason",
      "At the end of the check, every unresolved item should have a reason and a next action. Reasons include missing information, waiting on a vendor, needs approval, or outside the queue's scope. The reason should be short enough to scan and specific enough to act on. A handoff that says please review is not a handoff.",
      "Name the person who receives the item and the date it should move. Put the handoff in the same system as the work, not in a separate chat. The receiving person should not have to reconstruct the state from the inbox. A clean handoff is the difference between a queue and a pile.",
      "## Measure reconciliation drift",
      "Drift is the gap between what the inbox shows and what the source system shows. Count the items with a missing record, a missing message, a duplicate, or an owner mismatch. Track the count weekly rather than judging a single day. A small drift that grows is a signal that the matching rules need attention.",
      "Break drift down by work type and by person so the cause is visible. A spike after a new task type usually means the matching rule was never updated. A spike on one shift may mean the handoff is unclear. Fix one cause at a time and watch the next two weeks.",
      "## Keep the ritual short enough to survive",
      "A reconciliation that takes an hour will be skipped on a busy day. Aim for fifteen to thirty minutes on a normal queue. Use a saved view, a short form, and the same outcomes every time. Automate the count if the system allows it, but keep the judgment about missing records and duplicates with a person.",
      "Review the ritual after the first month and remove any field nobody uses. Keep the fields that changed a decision. Ask the specialists which step felt like paperwork and which step caught a real problem. A short check that people trust is more valuable than a thorough one they avoid."
    ]
